1
00:00:00,864 --> 00:00:04,835
A couple of years ago, I stood on the streets in eastern Ukraine,

2
00:00:04,835 --> 00:00:09,239
in Kramatorsk, in the aftermath of a Russian cruise missile strike

3
00:00:09,239 --> 00:00:12,776
on a residential apartment building.

4
00:00:12,776 --> 00:00:16,914
I remember the front of the building was blown off.

5
00:00:16,914 --> 00:00:20,350
You could see a living room and a kitchen inside, belongings burnt,

6
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:22,853
scattered on the floor, mixed in with the rubble.

7
00:00:22,853 --> 00:00:26,323
There were some weapons inspectors on one side,

8
00:00:26,323 --> 00:00:28,425
an excavator in the middle,

9
00:00:28,425 --> 00:00:30,260
and of course, in amongst it all,

10
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:34,464
the journalists were there, trying to interview those involved.

11
00:00:34,464 --> 00:00:39,236
One lady in particular had soot on her face.

12
00:00:39,236 --> 00:00:40,637
It was very moving to watch.

13
00:00:40,637 --> 00:00:42,706
She gathered her belongings from the rubble,

14
00:00:42,706 --> 00:00:44,207
and she put them on the floor,

15
00:00:44,207 --> 00:00:46,677
and she sat on them for safekeeping.

16
00:00:46,677 --> 00:00:51,949
These are the sorts of scenes that are broadcast around the world every night.

17
00:00:51,949 --> 00:00:53,784
And I sat and drew this image --

18
00:00:53,784 --> 00:00:56,286
that’s her in the black at the front.

19
00:00:56,286 --> 00:00:58,622
I found them to be so moving.

20
00:00:58,622 --> 00:01:05,896
And let me tell you -- the destruction is truly captivating.

21
00:01:05,896 --> 00:01:08,131
But around the back of the same building,

22
00:01:08,131 --> 00:01:12,402
I found a library of books had been blasted into a rose garden,

23
00:01:12,402 --> 00:01:18,775
and in amongst the thorns and the roses stood Petro, age 70.

24
00:01:18,775 --> 00:01:21,945
On his morning walk, he couldn't go any further.

25
00:01:21,945 --> 00:01:25,248
He felt it was his duty to stop, pick up the books,

26
00:01:25,248 --> 00:01:28,452
stack them in neat little piles.

27
00:01:28,452 --> 00:01:30,721
And as I drew him, he challenged me,

28
00:01:30,721 --> 00:01:33,123
and he said "I was brought up

29
00:01:33,123 --> 00:01:36,326
that if I see a loaf of bread on the floor, I'll pick it up.

30
00:01:36,326 --> 00:01:37,794
It's the same with books.

31
00:01:37,794 --> 00:01:40,597
They are food for the soul."

32
00:01:40,597 --> 00:01:44,368
I've drawn mass graves in Ukraine,

33
00:01:44,368 --> 00:01:47,004
a cesarean in Afghanistan

34
00:01:47,004 --> 00:01:49,339
and the war in Syria.

35
00:01:49,339 --> 00:01:53,543
And over two decades, I’ve had one realization over and over,

36
00:01:53,543 --> 00:01:56,546
that there are many truths to war.

37
00:01:56,546 --> 00:02:01,885
But on that morning in Kramatorsk, in Ukraine, it was so stark.

38
00:02:01,885 --> 00:02:04,021
It was Petro that captured my heart,

39
00:02:04,021 --> 00:02:09,059
this gentle soul standing amidst atrocity.

40
00:02:09,059 --> 00:02:10,727
This is what I do.

41
00:02:10,727 --> 00:02:15,032
I draw for magazines and newspapers, in ink, on location.

42
00:02:15,032 --> 00:02:18,635
And over weeks and weeks in Ukraine,

43
00:02:18,635 --> 00:02:23,006
it became my mission to be slow, to delve deeper.

44
00:02:23,006 --> 00:02:28,745
And I found that the news can tell us what happened,

45
00:02:28,745 --> 00:02:32,349
but the drawing can show us how it felt.

46
00:02:32,349 --> 00:02:37,521
In Kyiv, I met Madame Olga, aged 99.

47
00:02:37,521 --> 00:02:42,025
She was too old to get out of her bed when the air-raid sirens rang.

48
00:02:42,025 --> 00:02:45,062
I remember she had a Bible tucked under her pillow.

49
00:02:45,062 --> 00:02:48,432
She was very muddled, she was confusing Putin and Hitler,

50
00:02:48,432 --> 00:02:52,936
and she held the translator's hands about six inches from her face.

51
00:02:52,936 --> 00:02:57,641
And in this moment of wonderful clarity, she said,

52
00:02:57,641 --> 00:03:01,611
"If you be quiet, I will tell you the story from the beginning."

53
00:03:01,611 --> 00:03:04,147
She was born in 1923.

54
00:03:04,147 --> 00:03:07,184
She was taken to a collective farm with her father.

55
00:03:07,184 --> 00:03:09,052
She survived the Holodomor --

56
00:03:09,052 --> 00:03:12,522
the great famine which killed four million Ukrainians.

57
00:03:12,522 --> 00:03:18,028
When World War II started, she worked as a slave for a Nazi family.

58
00:03:18,028 --> 00:03:22,099
She recounted the story about one time they fed her worms.

59
00:03:22,099 --> 00:03:25,368
She ran away, on another occasion, to the police station,

60
00:03:25,368 --> 00:03:28,772
but the policeman returned her straight back to the house.

61
00:03:28,772 --> 00:03:33,677
When the war ended, she began to walk home to Ukraine.

62
00:03:33,677 --> 00:03:37,380
She met her husband on the way -- future husband, I should say.

63
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:38,615
They got married.

64
00:03:38,615 --> 00:03:39,816
She had four children.

65
00:03:39,816 --> 00:03:42,152
She lived another 80 years.

66
00:03:42,152 --> 00:03:44,588
If it hadn't been for the process of drawing,

67
00:03:44,588 --> 00:03:46,790
I wouldn’t have shared this moment with Olga,

68
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:48,892
and I wouldn't be able to share it with you.

69
00:03:48,892 --> 00:03:51,695
She died a couple of months after we met,

70
00:03:51,695 --> 00:03:56,733
peacefully, in her sleep, before her 100th birthday.

71
00:03:56,733 --> 00:04:01,538
I believe the news is failing us.

72
00:04:01,538 --> 00:04:06,376
It promotes destruction before humanity.

73
00:04:06,376 --> 00:04:08,945
It's polarizing and fatiguing.

74
00:04:08,945 --> 00:04:11,214
It gives the illusion of certainty.

75
00:04:11,214 --> 00:04:15,118
You might feel helpless or hopeless.

76
00:04:15,118 --> 00:04:18,722
We'd certainly be forgiven for turning it off.

77
00:04:18,722 --> 00:04:23,226
My antidote is to wander the streets and stand on corners.

78
00:04:23,226 --> 00:04:26,463
I work with local translators and fixers

79
00:04:26,463 --> 00:04:30,100
to interview the unsung and the understated.

80
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:32,302
Like Oleg, 23-year-old

81
00:04:32,302 --> 00:04:36,506
mixed martial arts fighter and football fan from Ukraine.

82
00:04:36,506 --> 00:04:40,911
He joined the military to fight the Russians with his football friends.

83
00:04:40,911 --> 00:04:44,514
But on this day, I drew him being served borscht

84
00:04:44,514 --> 00:04:48,318
by his mother, Lisa, the English teacher from that town,

85
00:04:48,318 --> 00:04:51,588
and he posed for me with his Kalashnikov,

86
00:04:51,588 --> 00:04:55,492
but also, more unusually, with his guinea pig, Almond.

87
00:04:55,492 --> 00:04:57,294
(Laughter)

88
00:04:57,294 --> 00:05:02,799
And throughout the sitting, he whispered to Almond affectionately,

89
00:05:02,799 --> 00:05:04,267
"You are a big pig."

90
00:05:04,267 --> 00:05:06,369
(Laughter)

91
00:05:06,369 --> 00:05:07,637
"You are a big pig."

92
00:05:07,637 --> 00:05:12,309
(Laughter)

93
00:05:12,309 --> 00:05:15,946
It's people like Sergei that remind me that these stories are continuing

94
00:05:15,946 --> 00:05:17,547
as I stand in front of you.

95
00:05:17,547 --> 00:05:20,083
Perhaps the most ordinary of them all.

96
00:05:20,083 --> 00:05:26,423
An architect whose house fell on his head when it was hit by a Russian missile.

97
00:05:26,423 --> 00:05:30,560
And I met him in the hospital, bandage wrapped around his head.

98
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:34,464
I asked if I could draw him and the nurse translated.

99
00:05:34,464 --> 00:05:38,201
And he looked at me down his nose and he grinned from ear to ear, and said,

100
00:05:38,201 --> 00:05:40,871
"Yes, but I will also draw you."

101
00:05:40,871 --> 00:05:42,272
(Laughter)

102
00:05:42,272 --> 00:05:46,209
And, very formally, a date was arranged for the next day,

103
00:05:46,209 --> 00:05:48,745
and I came back and we sat in one of the wardrooms.

104
00:05:48,745 --> 00:05:52,282
The windows were still sellotaped up in case there was an explosion.

105
00:05:52,282 --> 00:05:54,184
And we sat and we drew each other.

106
00:05:54,184 --> 00:05:57,120
We didn't share any common language.

107
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,890
We were both shy about our drawings.

108
00:05:59,890 --> 00:06:06,029
Like all good artists, he blamed his tools for the quality of his picture.

109
00:06:06,029 --> 00:06:12,235
And he'd probably be secretly proud to know that I'm showing you this.

110
00:06:12,235 --> 00:06:13,970
(Audience aws)

111
00:06:13,970 --> 00:06:19,409
(Applause)

112
00:06:19,409 --> 00:06:22,712
Sergei, this is your story.

113
00:06:22,712 --> 00:06:27,117
Of course, these are only four stories from Ukraine.

114
00:06:27,117 --> 00:06:32,756
But over the years, in Syria and Tajikistan, Gaza and Iraq,

115
00:06:32,756 --> 00:06:37,494
I have met and drawn hundreds of other people.

116
00:06:37,494 --> 00:06:40,196
But why, in the age of immediate news,

117
00:06:40,196 --> 00:06:43,533
do you need to know about Almond, the guinea pig,

118
00:06:43,533 --> 00:06:45,368
or Sergei the architect

119
00:06:45,368 --> 00:06:48,371
or Olga, the great-great-grandmother?

120
00:06:48,371 --> 00:06:52,676
Or Hamdo, Lisa, Dimitri?

121
00:06:52,676 --> 00:06:54,911
Well perhaps it’s obvious:

122
00:06:54,911 --> 00:06:59,449
I believe they could so easily have been one of us.

123
00:06:59,449 --> 00:07:03,820
They are the true consequences of war.

124
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:06,756
And I tell you about them, I share them with you tonight

125
00:07:06,756 --> 00:07:12,595
because they are ordinary people who had terror poured on their lives

126
00:07:12,595 --> 00:07:16,633
and resisted it with their hearts.

127
00:07:16,633 --> 00:07:21,638
I would like to finish with a confession.

128
00:07:21,638 --> 00:07:23,707
More of an admission, if you like,

129
00:07:23,707 --> 00:07:26,977
but a difficult one for artists, I think, to admit.

130
00:07:26,977 --> 00:07:31,381
As it turns out, it isn't really about the painting at all.

131
00:07:31,381 --> 00:07:37,620
That's just an excuse to sit a little longer, to give time.

132
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:39,789
We've heard about listening accurately today.

133
00:07:39,789 --> 00:07:41,658
This is about observing,

134
00:07:41,658 --> 00:07:43,827
seeing properly.

135
00:07:43,827 --> 00:07:46,696
Drawing is the only way I know how to do that.

136
00:07:46,696 --> 00:07:50,800
But today, I invite you --

137
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:53,069
more than that actually, if I'm allowed --

138
00:07:53,069 --> 00:07:57,974
I urge you to witness these stories and not consume them.

139
00:07:57,974 --> 00:08:00,443
To be deliberately slow

140
00:08:00,443 --> 00:08:04,247
and to cherish human connection.

141
00:08:04,247 --> 00:08:05,548
Thank you for listening.

142
00:08:05,548 --> 00:08:09,548
(Cheers and applause)
